You are working inside a production-grade Go repository.

Follow these architectural and engineering rules strictly.

------------------------------------------------------------
ARCHITECTURE PRINCIPLES
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Follow Clean Architecture (Hexagonal / Ports & Adapters).

Dependency direction must always flow inward:

handler → service → domain
            ↓
        repository (interface)
            ↓
     infrastructure (implementation)

Domain must NEVER depend on:
- HTTP frameworks
- Database drivers
- Redis
- Kafka
- External services
- Logging frameworks

Domain must contain only:
- Entities
- Value objects
- Business rules
- Interfaces (ports)

------------------------------------------------------------
PROJECT STRUCTURE RULES
------------------------------------------------------------

- cmd/ contains entry points only.
- internal/domain contains pure business logic.
- internal/service contains application use cases.
- internal/repository contains repository interfaces.
- internal/infrastructure contains external implementations.
- internal/handler contains transport layer logic (HTTP, gRPC, etc).

Do not move business logic into handler or infrastructure layers.

------------------------------------------------------------
DEPENDENCY INJECTION
------------------------------------------------------------

- Use constructor injection.
- Do not use global variables.
- Do not use hidden singletons.
- All dependencies must be explicit.

Example:
    func NewUserService(repo UserRepository) *UserService

------------------------------------------------------------
CONTEXT USAGE
------------------------------------------------------------

- Any function that touches:
    - network
    - database
    - filesystem
    - external service
must accept context.Context as the first parameter.

Never create background contexts inside business logic.

------------------------------------------------------------
INTERFACES
------------------------------------------------------------

- Define interfaces at the consumer side.
- Keep interfaces small and focused.
- Do not create “god interfaces”.

------------------------------------------------------------
ERROR HANDLING
------------------------------------------------------------

- Always wrap errors with context.
- Do not ignore errors.
- Do not panic in business logic.
- Panic only during startup failures.

------------------------------------------------------------
LOGGING
------------------------------------------------------------

- Use structured logging.
- Do not log inside domain layer.
- Logging belongs to infrastructure or handler layers.
- Do not use fmt.Println in production code.

------------------------------------------------------------
TESTING
------------------------------------------------------------

- Write unit tests alongside code.
- Mock via interfaces only.
- Do not mock concrete types.
- No real DB calls in unit tests.

------------------------------------------------------------
CONCURRENCY
------------------------------------------------------------

- Never start goroutines without clear lifecycle control.
- Respect context cancellation.
- Avoid shared mutable state.
- Prefer channels for coordination.
- Use sync primitives carefully.

------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIGURATION
------------------------------------------------------------

- All configuration must come from environment variables or config files.
- Validate config at startup.
- Fail fast if configuration is invalid.

------------------------------------------------------------
GENERAL CODE RULES
------------------------------------------------------------

- Keep packages small and focused.
- No circular dependencies.
- Avoid files larger than 300 lines.
- Keep functions short and readable.
- Prefer explicit over clever.
- Avoid premature abstraction.
- No utility dumping ground packages.

------------------------------------------------------------
PERFORMANCE & SAFETY
------------------------------------------------------------

- Avoid unnecessary allocations.
- Pass large structs by pointer.
- Do not expose internal state.
- Validate all external inputs at handler layer.

------------------------------------------------------------
FINAL PRINCIPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Clarity > Cleverness.
Explicit > Magic.
Maintainability > Short-term speed.

This is production infrastructure code.
Act accordingly.